- How would you extend to optionally grow the stack on demand?
- What testing strategy ensures no collateral damage when multiple threads fault simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical understanding of timer-driven thread wakeups, race condition handling, and efficient data structures in a kernel-like environment. It tests a candidate's ability to design a concrete mechanism that works correctly under contention while maintaining precise timing guarantees.

## Key Concepts

- Sleep queues and timer interrupt handling
- Min-heap data structure for efficient wake-time ordering
- Concurrency safety and time rechecking to mitigate timer jitter
- Correctness guarantees: ensuring no missed wakeups and exact timing

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal sketch of a per-CPU sleep queue
typedef struct SleepEntry {
  uint64_t wake_at;
  int tid;
  struct SleepEntry *next;
} SleepEntry;

// Enqueue thread for timed sleep
void sleep(int tid, uint64_t duration) {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  SleepEntry entry = {now + duration, tid};
  minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
  block_thread(tid);
}

// Timer interrupt handler
void timer_interrupt() {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  while (!minheap_empty(&cpu->sleep_queue)) {
    SleepEntry entry = minheap_peek(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at > now) break;

    entry = minheap_pop(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at <= now) {
      wake_thread(entry.tid);
    } else {
      minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explain real-world scheduler contention with many cores and cache locality. This tests lock-free design, memory ordering, and correctness under races.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free data structures
- Memory ordering (acquire/release)
- ABA problem and version counters
- Work-stealing guarantees and starvation avoidance

## Code Example
```c
// sketch
typedef struct {
  _Atomic int head;
  _Atomic int tail;
  void *buf[DEQUE_SIZE];
} Deque;

static inline void push(Deque *d, void *task) {
  int t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&d->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  d->buf[t & (DEQUE_SIZE-1)] = task;
}

static inline void *pop(Deque *d) {
  int t = atomic_fetch_sub_explicit(&d->tail, 1, memory_order_acq_rel);
  int h = atomic_load_explicit(&d->head, memory_order_acquire);
  if (t <= h) return NULL;
  return d->buf[(t-1) & (DEQUE_SIZE-1)];
}

static inline int steal(Deque *d, void **out) {
  int h = atomic_load_explicit(&d->head, memory_order_acquire);
  int t = atomic_load_explicit(&d->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h >= t) return 0;
  void *candidate = d->buf[h & (DEQUE_SIZE-1)];
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(&d->head, &h, h+1, memory_order_acq_rel, memory_order_acquire)) {
    *out = candidate;
    return 1;
  }
  return 0;
}
```

